TESTDATA["pet3-t3"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 3 · Test 3","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Roberto is suggesting that","options":["Sven is unavailable for a meeting next week.","he will join Sam in a meeting next Thursday.","Sam should invite Sven to their next meeting."],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Kaye and Brian found their drive to the hotel stressful.","Kaye and Brian’s car needs to be repaired before they leave.","Kaye and Brian will drive straight back after their hotel stay."],"answer":"A"},{"n":3,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Staff must tell the office when they arrive.","People visiting the school should inform the office.","School reports are available in the office."],"answer":"B"},{"n":4,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Tim’s mother wants him to get some","options":["a salad.","bread.","eggs."],"answer":"B"},{"n":5,"text":"","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["Snacks are available before the performance.","Help yourself to drinks after the performance.","You can have a drink during the break."],"answer":"C"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Mark","text":"Mark is an engineer and would like to spend a day looking at modern inventions and scientific discoveries. His 12-year-old daughter will probably go with him.","answer":"G"},{"n":7,"name":"Sarah Jones","text":"Sarah Jones and her young son want to spend a day out and see some of the animals that Australia is famous for. Sarah also wants to buy presents to take home.","answer":"B"},{"n":8,"name":"Susan","text":"Before she leaves Australia, Susan wants to spend a day shopping. She is looking for something special for her father, who is interested in art. She dislikes large shopping centres.","answer":"F"},{"n":9,"name":"Mario","text":"Mario is hoping to spend six months touring round the whole of Australia. Before setting off he wants to find out as much as possible about its history and inhabitants.","answer":"C"},{"n":10,"name":"Stefan Holmstrom","text":"Stefan Holmstrom is interested in modern buildings, and always likes to get to know as much as possible about them. He also enjoys listening to music.","answer":"D"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"The Harbourside Development","text":"is one of the world’s most exciting places to shop. It’s situated right on the edge of Sydney Harbour, and there are over 200 shops, selling everything from home-made sweets to the very latest in fashion; from Australian souvenirs to Swiss watches and Japanese cameras."},{"letter":"B","title":"The Australian Wildlife Park","text":"has been specially built to give people an extra close look at Australia’s native animals. Have your photo taken with a kangaroo, touch and feed koala bears. There are talks every afternoon about these animals, which are only found in this part of the world. Excellent souvenir shop."},{"letter":"C","title":"A visit to the Australian Museum","text":"is like an adventure across Australia. There are exhibitions on the people and their way of life, and the whole continent. Talks and guided tours help to make your visit unforgettable. There’s also a café and a good bookshop, selling excellent maps."},{"letter":"D","title":"The Opera House","text":"This is a 20th century masterpiece of engineering and offers top international performances. A guided tour lasts two hours and is available any day between 9 am and 4 pm. Special ‘backstage’ tours are available on Sundays."},{"letter":"E","title":"The State Library","text":"is in the oldest part of Sydney. There are free film screenings, guided tours and educational activities. It has regular exhibitions of books on science and technology. Learn to use a micro-computer, watch a video, or look through the Library Shop."},{"letter":"F","title":"The Rocks","text":"Surrounded by the modern city, the Rocks is the oldest part of Sydney and full of history. On weekends there are outdoor concerts and street theatre to enjoy. Every Saturday and Sunday there is a market where it is possible to buy the works of local artists."},{"letter":"G","title":"The Powerhouse Museum","text":"One of the world’s leading museums, the Powerhouse Museum has over 25 exhibitions on the latest developments in science and technology. Everything from a NASA space station to the first car ever built can be found at this museum. There are also many activities for children here."},{"letter":"H","title":"Taronga Zoo","text":"has Australia’s finest collection of rainforest birds and a sea-life centre which has many of the fish that can be found off the coasts of Australia and New Zealand. Taronga is also famous for its large group of South East Asian monkeys. Snacks are available at the café."}]},{"part":3,"title":"Gallery News","text":"Easter quiz for children\nDuring the Easter holidays young visitors can take part in a quiz on the subject of Surprises. The free quiz sheets can be picked up from the desk at the Orange Street entrance. Children will be directed to 14 surprising paintings and asked a variety of questions about them. The quiz sheets will be available during normal gallery hours from 25 March until 9 April.\n\nNew picture\nThe Gallery now has a fine still life by the Dutch 18th century painter Jan van Os. This large picture (89.1 × 71 cm) of flowers and fruit is painted in light, bright colours on wood. It is one of the first pictures of this type in the Gallery. The picture is signed and dated 1777 and 1778. It is not unusual for a picture like this to be dated in two years: the artist waited for particular flowers to bloom in their different seasons in order to paint them. The picture was generously given to the Gallery by Miss Violet Churchman in memory of her sister Ida Nancy. It is now on display in Room 25.\n\nSpecial exhibition\nThe exhibition ‘Painting in Spain during the late 18th century’ opened in the Sunley Room on 15 March. Recently the Gallery has bought works by three Spanish painters of this period: Paret, Melendez and Francisco Bayeu, who are the focus of the exhibition. These three artists are joined by Francisco’s brother Ramon, by Antonio Gonzalez, and by two Italians who worked in Spain during these years – Corrado Giaquinto and Giovanni Battista Tiepolo. The exhibition runs until 31 May.\n\nLecture news\nThe series of lectures arranged to go with the special exhibition continues every Wednesday. On 5 April Lizzie Barker will discuss the work of Melendez, while on 12 April Sarah Symmons will lecture on Luis Paret. On 19 and 26 April Juliet Wilson will talk about Francisco Bayeu. On Tuesdays in April, Erika Langmuir will explain how artists often ‘tell a story’ through their pictures.\n\nGallery restaurant improvements\nThe Gallery is delighted to announce that an improved and expanded service is now available in the Gallery restaurant. With its new kitchens the restaurant offers a wide choice of hot and cold dishes at reasonable prices.\n\nInformation for visitors\nFree guided tours of the Gallery take place every day at 11.00 am and 3.00 pm (except Sundays). These tours introduce some of the Gallery’s greatest pictures, and show the whole range of the Gallery’s collection of some of the most important European paintings from the 13th to the early 20th centuries.","items":[{"n":11,"q":"Children can answer quiz questions about some specially chosen pictures.","answer":"A"},{"n":12,"q":"The Gallery has few pictures like their new one by Jan van Os.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"It took Jan van Os a lot of time to complete his picture.","answer":"A"},{"n":14,"q":"The Gallery had to pay a lot for the new picture.","answer":"B"},{"n":15,"q":"The special exhibition has pictures by Spanish artists and by other artists who worked in Spain.","answer":"A"},{"n":16,"q":"The Easter quiz is on for longer than the special exhibition.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"In the Gallery on any Wednesday in April you can learn about 18th century Spanish painters.","answer":"A"},{"n":18,"q":"The Gallery has just opened a restaurant for visitors.","answer":"B"},{"n":19,"q":"A guide takes visitors round the Gallery twice every day.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"q":"You can see paintings from all over the world in the Gallery.","answer":"B"}]},{"part":4,"title":"","text":"When I injured my back I had to take a break from my running career. I decided to introduce more women to the sport, to show them how much fun it can be and to give them the confidence to get out and run.\n\nI decided to start a running club for women in my area because I was annoyed by the attitude of many race organisers. They complain about the lack of women in the sport but also use this as an excuse for not providing separate changing facilities.\n\nI put up posters and 40 women, young and old, fit and unfit, joined. All of them were attracted by the idea of losing weight but I don’t think they had really thought about running before. When or if they did, they had a picture of painful training. They didn’t think of chatting and smiling while running in beautiful places, like by a river.\n\nAt first they ran for only a minute – now they can run for thirty minutes. They’ve also learned from other runners about diet and keeping fit in general.\n\nI wanted to do something for women’s running and I’ve had so much pleasure watching their progress – almost as much as they’ve had themselves.","items":[{"n":21,"q":"What is the writer’s main aim in writing the text?","options":["to describe her own running career","to complain about race organisers","to talk about women runners","to describe good running methods"],"answer":"C"},{"n":22,"q":"What would a reader find out from the text?","options":["the best kinds of places for running","how runners can avoid injuring themselves","the progress made by the women in the club","the teaching skills of the writer"],"answer":"C"},{"n":23,"q":"What is the writer’s opinion of the runners she trained?","options":["They were too serious.","They needed encouraging.","They couldn’t develop their skills.","They were difficult to train."],"answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"The women joined the running club to","options":["have a good time.","meet other people.","help them lose weight.","become top runners."],"answer":"C"},{"n":25,"q":"Which of the following would be the best title for the club poster?","options":["Discover the pleasures of running","Riverside Running Club for Women","Athletics competitions: how to win","KEEP FIT BY TRAINING HARD"],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":5,"title":"MONEY","text":"What is money? The pound, the dollar or the euro are actually just (0) ……… (like) a gram or a kilometre. The difference is that you can exchange money for something [26]. A ten pound note may buy a book, a huge bag of sweets, or a [27] of cinema tickets. But the note itself is only a printed [28] of paper which costs almost nothing to make. Thousands of years [29], people didn’t have money as we know [30]. There were no banks [31] even shops. In those days Mr Green the farmer exchanged the corn he [32] grown for Mr Hive’s honey. This was an exchange arranged between two [33], each of whom had something that the other wanted. But in time, most societies invented their own ‘currencies’ [34] that people could exchange more. The different currencies began to join together, which is why [35] everyone uses the same currency in their country.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["other","else","another","apart"],"answer":"B"},"27":{"options":["couple","double","few","several"],"answer":"A"},"28":{"options":["slice","part","side","piece"],"answer":"D"},"29":{"options":["since","past","before","ago"],"answer":"D"},"30":{"options":["them","it","some","that"],"answer":"B"},"31":{"options":["or","neither","and","but"],"answer":"A"},"32":{"options":["did","was","had","has"],"answer":"C"},"33":{"options":["jobs","people","things","goods"],"answer":"B"},"34":{"options":["for","by","because","so"],"answer":"D"},"35":{"options":["tomorrow","today","recently","soon"],"answer":"B"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some questions about cold weather.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"Last year wasn’t as cold as this year.","before":"This year is","after":"last year was.","answers":["colder than"]},{"n":2,"first":"How much is it to go skiing?","before":"What","after":"to go skiing?","answers":["does it cost"]},{"n":3,"first":"Do you know who these skis belong to?","before":"Do you know","after":"these skis are?","answers":["whose"]},{"n":4,"first":"James can ski well.","before":"James is","after":"skiing.","answers":["good at","very good at","brilliant at","fantastic at","great at","excellent at"]},{"n":5,"first":"Driving in heavy snow isn’t easy.","before":"Heavy snow makes it","after":"drive.","answers":["difficult to","hard to","less easy to"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"You want to invite Maria, an English-speaking student who is staying with you, to go out tonight with you and your friends.\n\nWrite a note to leave for Maria\n• inviting her to go out with you all tonight\n• saying where you plan to spend the evening\n• suggesting what time she should be ready.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["invitare Maria a uscire","dire dove si pensa di passare la serata","suggerire a che ora deve essere pronta"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"story","task":"You have to write a story for your English homework.\nYour story must begin with this sentence:\n\nThe ship left the harbour at sunset.\n\nWrite your story in about 100 words on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from an English friend.\n\n“In your last letter, you said you had made some changes to your room. What does it look like now? What new furniture have you got? Tell me all about it!”\n\nNow write a letter telling your friend about your room.\nWrite your letter in about 100 words on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet3-t3-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"How did the woman get to work today?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"q":"Where does the pollution come from?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":3,"q":"When must the boys get on the coach?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":4,"q":"What fruit do they take?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":5,"q":"Which present has the man bought?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"Where are the photographs?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":7,"q":"What did Ben break?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"}],"example":"img/pet3-t3-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear a radio presenter talking about weekend events in the Carlisle area.","audio":"audio/pet3-t3-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"The concert starts at","options":["7 pm.","7.30 pm.","8.30 pm."],"answer":"B"},{"n":9,"q":"The money from the concert will pay for","options":["books for the school library.","some school sports equipment.","a school trip to London."],"answer":"B"},{"n":10,"q":"The Walking Club will meet","options":["in the market square.","at the bus station.","in the car park by the lake."],"answer":"C"},{"n":11,"q":"You can hear a talk by a writer","options":["in the library.","at the bookshop.","at the university."],"answer":"A"},{"n":12,"q":"The writer will talk about","options":["a famous person she has written about.","a recent journey she has made.","the next book she will write."],"answer":"B"},{"n":13,"q":"To go to the talk you should","options":["be a club member.","be over 16.","book a ticket."],"answer":"C"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a recorded message about hotels in the National Park.","audio":"audio/pet3-t3-l3.mp3","notes":"HOTELS IN THE NATIONAL PARK\n\nThe Marston Hotel\nGood for people who like [14] .\nIf you ask, the hotel will make you a [15] .\n\nThe Bristol Hotel\nPrice includes [16] .\n\nThe Ferndale Hotel\nGood view of [17] .\n\nFirtrees Hotel\nHas won prizes for its [18] .\nPrice of a double room [19] £ .......... a night.","answers":{"14":["walking","walk","to walk","walks"],"15":["picnic","lunch","picnic lunch"],"16":["dinner","dinners"],"17":["lake","the lake","lakeside","the lakeside"],"18":["cooking","food","English cooking","English food"],"19":["185","£185"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a conversation between a boy, John, and a girl, Louise.","audio":"audio/pet3-t3-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"John hopes to keep his singing lessons a secret.","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"q":"John says he has had singing lessons before.","answer":"A"},{"n":22,"q":"John and Louise agree that the teacher is strict.","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"John has always liked musical shows.","answer":"B"},{"n":24,"q":"John was pleased with the practice show.","answer":"A"},{"n":25,"q":"Louise would like to dance in a musical show.","answer":"B"}]}]};
